# Templates

A **template** is what `grem init` and `grem upgrade` stamp from: a directory
holding a `bootstrap.py` manifest and a `content/` tree. `bootstrap.py` is a
trusted Python module grem evaluates to discover the project structure; the files
are copied from `content/`, with template variables substituted (see below).

Bundled templates live at `src/grem/templates/<name>/` and `--template` selects
one by directory name — there is no registry to update. Two ship today:
`python` (the default) and `rust`. They stamp the same control layer and differ
only where the language does: `pyproject.toml` and `src/{{ package_name }}/`
against `Cargo.toml`, `rust-toolchain.toml`, `.gitignore`, and compiling
`src/lib.rs` + `src/main.rs` stubs. Each seeds its own `doc/stack.md`.

A declared file's bytes must come from its own template's `content/` — the
planner rejects any source that escapes it — so the shared harness prompts,
doc styles, wiki cards, and agent instruction files are **duplicated per
template**. Editing one of those files means editing both copies.

## The manifest

`bootstrap.py` declares three module-level names:

- **`NAME`** — the template's identity, matched against `.grem/config.yaml`.
- **`VERSION`** — a SemVer 2 string; must equal the `template_version` in
  `content/.grem/config.yaml` (see [[grem-config]]).
- **`ROOT`** — the top **`Moniker`** class whose declarations describe the tree.

## Monikers declare the tree

A **`Moniker`** is a class whose annotated methods each declare one output entry.
Two decorators mark them:

- **`@folder`** — a subfolder; the method's return annotation names the child
  `Moniker` (an empty `Moniker` is a leaf directory). `@folder("name")` sets the
  emitted folder name explicitly.
- **`@file("name")`** — a file copied from the matching path under `content/`
  (with variables substituted); a `source=` argument overrides the source path.
  A `@folder("{{ package_name }}")` or `@file` name may itself contain a token,
  so declarations like `src/{{ package_name }}` resolve to `src/<package_name>`.

Declaration method **bodies are never executed** — grem reads only the
annotations, so a template cannot run arbitrary code during scaffolding.

## Variables (Mustache interpolation)

Template content and declared names reference **variables** as `{{ name }}`
tokens. `scaffold` substitutes them using the Mustache *interpolation subset*
only — no sections, partials, or inheritance — and inserts values raw (HTML
escaping is off, since templates hold code and config). Unknown tokens are left
untouched. `grem init` resolves the variables from the project name and records
them in [[grem-config]]; `grem upgrade` reads them back so refreshed files
re-render with the same values.

## Ownership: `.gremignore`

A template ships a root **`.gremignore`** listing the paths that are *project-owned*
— seeded once at `init`, then never re-stamped. `grem upgrade` refreshes only the
*template-owned* control layer (harness prompts, styles, `.grem/**`) and skips
every path matched by `.gremignore` (gitignore-style globs, `**`, trailing `/`).
`.gremignore` is always protected implicitly, so user edits to it survive. In the
`python` template the source tree, `README.md`, `pyproject.toml`, the proposals
index, the Kanban board, and `doc/memory/` are project-owned; the `rust` template
adds `tests/**`, `Cargo.toml`, `rust-toolchain.toml`, and `.gitignore` in place of
`pyproject.toml`. `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` are template-owned and refreshed in
both (put custom rules under `doc/memory/`).

## Deterministic materialization

`load_manifest` validates the module (`ROOT` is a `Moniker`; `NAME` and `VERSION`
are present and agree with the config), then `scaffold` plans and writes the
tree. Output ordering is canonical — sorted by path, with no timestamps or
machine-specific values — so the same template and variables always produce the
same bytes. `scaffold` refuses to overwrite existing files or re-stamp a
directory that already holds a `.grem` folder.

See [[grem-cli]] and [[grem-config]].
